.TH QFW-SETUP 1 "August 2026" "QFw 0.1.0" "QFw Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-setup \- prepare one QFw application runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-setup
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-setup
creates one application run directory, resolves site and runtime
configuration, records the generated environment, starts requested
application-owned services, and waits for the required directory service.
It prints the resolved run directory on success and marks that run as current.
.PP
For a local or hybrid profile, setup uses the same lifecycle engine as
.BR qfw-dir-svc (1)
and
.BR qfw-qpm-svc (1).
Each selected QPM has an independent manager run directory. An optional PRTE
DVM is owned by its QPM manager. A failure stops every application-owned
manager already started by the invocation.
.PP
For a selected service with
.B environment-modules
or
.BR required-executables ,
setup resolves the module environment before PRTE and QPM startup. The module
environment belongs to those managed processes and does not modify the calling
shell. Missing modules or executables fail setup before a service is started.
.PP
When the selected runtime uses site services, setup records and validates the
site directory connection but does not adopt or stop those services.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --site-config " file"
Use
.I file
instead of the activated site configuration.
.TP
.BI --runtime-config " file"
Use an explicit runtime YAML file.
.TP
.BI --profile " name"
Use the installed runtime profile named
.IR name .
.TP
.BI --service-id " id"
Start only service
.I id
from the local service manifest. This option requires a runtime profile or
configuration containing
.BR local-services .
.TP
.BI --run-id " id"
Use an explicit identifier instead of generating one.
.TP
.BI --run-dir " directory"
Create the runtime at an explicit path instead of below
.BR QFW_RUN_BASE_DIR .
.TP
.B --dry-run
Resolve configuration and write representative runtime state without starting
services or waiting for directory readiness.
.TP
.B --no-start-services
Prepare state without starting services. Unlike
.BR --dry-run ,
required directory readiness is still checked.
.SH GENERATED FILES
.TP
.I run-dir/state/runtime-state.json
Resolved configuration, allocation, environment, application-owned manager
records, setup state, and lifecycle timestamps.
.TP
.I run-dir/qfw-runtime-env.sh
Environment inherited by
.BR qfw-srun (1).
.TP
.I run-dir/service-plane/directory/
Application-owned directory manager state when requested.
.TP
.I run-dir/service-plane/qpm/service-id/
Application-owned QPM and optional DVM manager state for each selected service.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates complete setup and readiness. Configuration errors, service
startup failures, and directory readiness failures return nonzero.
